## Measurement stance (STANDING-ORDERS §7)

  The subject is **which** UDP broadcasts the DUT relays, so it cannot be graded on a ping
  or on `show running-config`. Every leg sends crafted frames with `tools/arpleg.py` from
  host eth3 (ingress segment) and counts what arrives on host eth4 (the far segment),
  decoding the IP source, IP destination, UDP destination port AND both MAC addresses of
  every frame.

  **Two controls in EVERY counted leg:**
   - a UDP flow on port **7777**, which is never in `ip forward-protocol`: it must NEVER
     be relayed. It is the discriminator that stops "a broadcast arrived" being mistaken
     for "the helper relayed it";
   - a routed **ICMP** unicast 10.38.22.40 -> 10.38.22.65, which must ALWAYS arrive on
     eth4: it proves in the same capture that the DUT is routing between the two segments
     and that the eth4 instrument is alive, so a zero for the UDP flows is a measurement.

  🔴 **Foreign-host exposure, handled.** 10.38.22.41 / 00:0c:25:2a:a5:53 is live on the
  INGRESS segment (10.38.22.32/27) and is not ours. The counted side of every leg is
  **eth4 / 10.38.22.64/27, which has no known foreign host**, so a stray cannot inflate a
  count. Every frame is additionally attributed by source and destination MAC, and the
  30 s background baseline taken for this group (T44181, same session) measured **0 ICMP
  and 0 UDP on eth4** and 2 ARP frames on eth3, none of them from the foreign host.

## STEP 0 (designed) — prove the commands exist, read-only

  `tools/qmark.py`, no trailing CR (work/s0-qmark-global.log, work/s0-qmark-if.log):

    awplus(config)#ip forward-protocol  ?
      udp  User Datagram Protocol(UDP)
    awplus(config)#ip forward-protocol udp  ?
      PORT  UDP Port number
    awplus(config-if)#ip helper-address  ?
      A.B.C.D  Forwarding destination address for IP Helper

  🟠 O-17 — `ip forward-protocol udp` takes a **numeric port only**. There are no named
  keywords on this platform (no `tftp`, `domain`, `netbios-ns`, `time`, ...), and `udp` is
  the only protocol the command accepts -- there is no `ip forward-protocol nd` or similar.
  A case written against the Cisco-style named forms would fail at its first command.
  `ip helper-address` takes exactly one `A.B.C.D` -- no interface form, no VRF keyword.
  VERDICT step 0: PASS, nothing UNSUPPORTED.

