## Findings and observations

  **No F-level defect.** Every sentence of the guide's IP Helper section that this bench
  can reach was confirmed in the dataplane. Two observations:

  🟠 O-17 — `ip forward-protocol` accepts only `udp`, and `ip forward-protocol udp` accepts
     only a numeric `PORT`. No named service keywords exist on this platform, and
     `ip helper-address` takes a bare `A.B.C.D` with no interface or VRF form. A test
     written against the named forms fails at its first command. Evidence:
     work/s0-qmark-global.log, work/s0-qmark-if.log.

  🟠 O-18 — a relayed frame keeps the ORIGINAL IP source address and BOTH UDP ports; only
     the IP destination and the Ethernet header are rewritten. IP Helper is a plain
     forwarder with no relay-agent semantics (no giaddr, no option insertion), which is the
     opposite of `ip dhcp-relay` on the same box (x960 §6). Worth stating because a server
     behind the helper sees the client's real address, and because a tester looking for a
     giaddr rewrite will think the relay is broken. Evidence: work/s2-helper-on.log.

  **Tool trap found and fixed generically (this one will bite the next case that uses a
  well-known port).** `arpleg.py` marked its own frames by looking for `CKARPLEG` in
  `pkt[Raw].load`. scapy dissects well-known UDP ports into their OWN layer -- 69 becomes
  TFTP, 67/68 BOOTP, 53 DNS -- so for the `:69` burst there is no `Raw` layer at all: all
  five of the tool's own frames reported as foreign and the leg exited 7 with
  "COUNTER DISAGREEMENT: sent 20 marked frame(s), capture on eth3 holds 15". The
  measurement was unaffected but the self-check was wrong, which is worse. Both the
  `classify()` mark and the `report()` counter now scan `bytes(pkt)`, the whole frame.
  Same family as the documented `dhcp4.py` BOOTP trap. Re-ran the baseline leg after the
  fix: all four flows `[ours]`, exit 0.

  **New generic capability in `tools/arpleg.py`** (README updated), needed by this case and
  by T44182 and reusable by anything that must send a specific UDP broadcast:
    `--udp DST[:PORT][,...]`  UDP probes with a CHOSEN destination port (the old `--probe`
                              hardcodes dport 9), so a matched port and an unmatched
                              control port ride in one leg;
    `--l2-dst MAC|broadcast`  the Ethernet destination for `--udp` ONLY -- `--probe` and
                              `--icmp` keep `--gw-mac`, which is what lets one leg carry a
                              link-layer broadcast flow AND a routed unicast control;
    `--sport N`, `--ttl N`.

## Run quality

  CLEAN. No confounder. Baseline OFF measured before any positive claim; two controls in
  every counted leg (an unhelped UDP port that must not be relayed, and a routed ICMP
  unicast that must arrive); the counted side deliberately placed on the segment with no
  foreign host; removal and reboot persistence both proven in the dataplane; teardown diff
  empty.

VERDICT: PASS -- no authored step text exists, so the case was designed from the IP Feature
Overview Guide (C613-22007-00 REV I p.16) and the DUT's CLI surface, and every guide claim
reachable on this bench was confirmed on the wire: nothing relayed by default; both
all-ones and subnet-directed UDP broadcasts relayed on a helped port while an unhelped port
was never relayed; the port list and the helper address proven independent; multiple helper
addresses each producing their own copy; a helper address that is a connected subnet's
broadcast address forwarded as a true link-layer broadcast; and removal and reboot
persistence both proven in the dataplane. No platform defect; two observations (O-17, O-18).
